phagia, which affects swallowing ability, further complicating the health of older individuals. Nutritional interventions, including increased protein intake and exercise, have been shown to mitigate the effects of sarcopenia, highlighting the importance of proper nutrition and physical activity in managing this condition.\n\nSarcopenia was first proposed by Irwin Rosenberg in 1989 and has since been the focus of numerous studies aimed at understanding its implications and developing effective interventions. It is recognized as a syndrome that not only impacts physical health but can also influence cognitive health and overall quality of life in the elderly population.\",83\r\n10180,POST-LIVER TRANSPLANTATION,\"Post-liver transplantation refers to the period after a liver transplant, where patients require careful nutritional management.\",5\r\n10154,MUSCLE STRENGTH,\"Muscle strength is a vital component in evaluating the nutritional status of patients with cirrhosis and plays a significant role in the assessment of sarcopenia. It is commonly measured using handgrip dynamometry, which is a standard method for quantifying muscle strength. Additionally, muscle strength can be assessed through various tests, including handgrip strength and knee extension. Overall, muscle strength serves as an important physical attribute that reflects an individual's health and functional capacity.\",5\r\n3321,JAPANESE WOMEN,Japanese women over 75 years old who participated in a study on sarcopenia and protein intake.,3\r\n3264,HANDGRIP STRENGTH,Handgrip strength is a specific measure of muscle strength that is often utilized as a diagnostic criterion for sarcopenia.,2\r\n12706,MUSCULO,\"Músculo refers to muscle tissue, which has a maximum storage limit for creatine. This tissue is influenced by supplementation, indicating that the amount of creatine that can be stored in muscle is finite and can be enhanced through dietary or supplemental means.\",2\r\n3554,AMINO ACID SUPPLEMENTATION,